          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "Your symptoms of a heavy squeezing feeling behind your breastbone when walking uphill, which eases with rest, require medical attention. While classified as routine in urgency, it is essential to speak with a healthcare professional for a thorough evaluation, particularly if the symptoms worsen or are new and unexplained.\n\nIf your symptoms are determined to be indicative of angina, prevention strategies generally include lifestyle changes, medications, and potential participation in cardiac rehabilitation. Key lifestyle measures involve quitting smoking, adhering to a heart-healthy diet rich in fruits, vegetables, and whole grains while low in saturated fats, engaging in regular aerobic physical activity, managing weight, and limiting alcohol intake. \n\nMedications that may be recommended include antiplatelet therapy and high-intensity statins to manage cholesterol levels. Cardiac rehabilitation, which involves structured supervised exercise and education, can also be a valuable part of managing cardiovascular risk factors. Additionally, dietary recommendations would focus on consuming nutrient-rich foods and minimizing processed foods and added sugars to help reduce the risk of further cardiovascular issues. \n\nPlease discuss these strategies with your healthcare provider to develop a personalized plan tailored to your health needs.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
